{"repo": "EschaLabs/Qwen3.8-27B-Escha-W2", "source_sha256": "3e8057f24bfaa4a5f96faca89a19de8adc8df043dc338517dda092c8f432634b", "candidates_sha256": "381a90a8cb8387d5644e2b3412ce17f8331c6641f9c4cd3f0f03369bb8fee40b", "candidates": {"c1": {"L": 201, "raw": "`0.72`", "header": "Default", "label": "`MEM`"}, "c2": {"L": 201, "raw": "Fraction of VRAM the weight + KV pool is carved from. **Too low fails too**, with \"Not enough memory \u2026 increase --mem-fraction-static\". If CUDA-graph capture OOMs, step *down* (0.70, 0.68), not up. Raise it on a card larger than 24 GB.", "header": "Notes", "label": "`MEM`"}, "c3": {"L": 202, "raw": "`65536`", "header": "Default", "label": "`CTXLEN`"}, "c4": {"L": 202, "raw": "Per-request context cap \u2014 a **default, not a ceiling**, and it allocates nothing by itself. What binds is the shared pool the server prints at startup as `max_total_num_tokens`, which must be \u2265 *concurrent streams \u00d7 context*. Raising `CTXLEN` without raising `MEM` gets you a pool too small for it and, with `TRUNCATE=1`, silent truncation. See **[Long context](#long-context)**.", "header": "Notes", "label": "`CTXLEN`"}, "c5": {"L": 203, "raw": "`0.3`", "header": "Default", "label": "`MAMBA_RATIO`"}, "c6.0": {"L": 203, "raw": "0.9", "header": "Notes", "label": "`MAMBA_RATIO`"}, "c6.1": {"L": 203, "raw": "0.3", "header": "Notes", "label": "`MAMBA_RATIO`"}, "c6.2": {"L": 203, "raw": "65536", "header": "Notes", "label": "`MAMBA_RATIO`"}, "c7": {"L": 204, "raw": "`1`", "header": "Default", "label": "`GRAPHS`"}, "c8": {"L": 204, "raw": "CUDA graphs. Mandatory for performance \u2014 this architecture runs many small kernels per token, so eager decode is launch-bound. `0` only to debug a capture failure.", "header": "Notes", "label": "`GRAPHS`"}, "c9.0": {"L": 205, "raw": "1", "header": "Default", "label": "`CUDA_GRAPH_BS`"}, "c9.1": {"L": 205, "raw": "2", "header": "Default", "label": "`CUDA_GRAPH_BS`"}, "c9.2": {"L": 205, "raw": "4", "header": "Default", "label": "`CUDA_GRAPH_BS`"}, "c9.3": {"L": 205, "raw": "8", "header": "Default", "label": "`CUDA_GRAPH_BS`"}, "c9.4": {"L": 205, "raw": "12", "header": "Default", "label": "`CUDA_GRAPH_BS`"}, "c9.5": {"L": 205, "raw": "16", "header": "Default", "label": "`CUDA_GRAPH_BS`"}, "c10": {"L": 205, "raw": "Batch sizes captured. **Must include your maximum batch size** or that batch silently runs eager (~15% low). Note the `12`/`16` entries do **not** take effect at the shipped `MEM`/`MAMBA_RATIO` \u2014 the recurrent pool clamps a 24 GB card to 8\u20139 streams and those entries are dropped. Raise `MAXREQ`/`MAXMAMBA`/`MEM` first, then extend to `\"1 2 4 8 12 16 24 32\"`.", "header": "Notes", "label": "`CUDA_GRAPH_BS`"}, "c11": {"L": 206, "raw": "`0`", "header": "Default", "label": "`RADIX`"}, "c12": {"L": 206, "raw": "Prefix caching, **off by default here** \u2014 the radix cache without speculative decoding disables the overlap scheduler, and on this hybrid it only serves **exact, complete** repeats (measured: [Long context](#long-context)). It does **not** speed up a conversation that grows, so it is not an agentic-latency lever. If you do set `1`, keep `MAXREQ` **and** `MAXMAMBA` \u2265 2 \u2014 prefix caching consumes a request slot, and either of them at `1` leaves `max_running_requests=0`, which empties the capture list and **fails at startup** with `AssertionError: capture_bs=[0]`.", "header": "Notes", "label": "`RADIX`"}, "c13": {"L": 207, "raw": "`1`", "header": "Default", "label": "`THINK`"}, "c14": {"L": 207, "raw": "`0` serves thinking-off by default. Either way, clients can flip it per request via `chat_template_kwargs`.", "header": "Notes", "label": "`THINK`"}, "c15": {"L": 266, "raw": "24 GB", "header": "VRAM", "label": "RTX 4090"}, "c16": {"L": 266, "raw": "sm_89", "header": "arch", "label": "RTX 4090"}, "c17": {"L": 266, "raw": "67.3 tok/s", "header": "1-user decode, no spec", "label": "RTX 4090"}, "c18": {"L": 266, "raw": "**129.3 tok/s**", "header": "**wi